{
 "cells": [
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "# Vachan V2 — Per-Persona Tone Dial (control vectors)\n\n**What this adds on top of the basic spike:** instead of a single generic Hinglish↔English axis built from hand-written prompts, we build the control vector from a **real persona's own anchor phrases** — the Hinglish messages they actually sent, paired with their English-translated versions. The resulting vector is *that person's* tone direction, not a generic one.\n\n**Why it matters:** two personas can both be \"casual Hinglish\", but their Hinglish sounds different. Rahul says \"yaar kya scene hai\"; a corporate-leaning persona says \"bhai meeting confirm hua?\". Building the vector from their anchors captures that difference and bakes it into the dial.\n\n**Pre-requisite:** the basic spike notebook (`hinglish_control_vector_kaggle.ipynb`) must have run green first. Same Kaggle setup — GPU T4, Internet On.\n\n> Runtime: ~8–12 min on a Kaggle **T4**."
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "## 0. Setup"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": "!pip install -q repeng transformers accelerate bitsandbytes"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": "import torch\nfrom transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM, AutoTokenizer, BitsAndBytesConfig\nfrom repeng import ControlVector, ControlModel, DatasetEntry\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json, os\n\nMODEL = \"NousResearch/Meta-Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct\"\n\ntokenizer = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(MODEL)\ntokenizer.pad_token_id = tokenizer.eos_token_id\n\nbnb = BitsAndBytesConfig(\n    load_in_4bit=True,\n    bnb_4bit_compute_dtype=torch.float16,\n    bnb_4bit_quant_type=\"nf4\",\n)\nmodel = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(MODEL, quantization_config=bnb, device_map=\"auto\")\n\n# Layer band: middle/late layers where style lives.\nmodel = ControlModel(model, list(range(-5, -18, -1)))\nprint(\"loaded:\", MODEL)"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "## 1. Persona Anchor Bank\n\nThis is where the per-persona data lives. In production Vachan, these come from the persona's stored message history. For this notebook, we simulate **three personas** so you can see the difference:\n\n- **Rahul** — young, gamer vibe, heavy Hinglish slang\n- **Priya** — urban professional, light code-mix\n- **Ankit** — small-town, more Hindi-dominant\n\nEach persona has `hinglish_anchors` (their real voice) and `english_anchors` (the same meaning in clean English — used as the negative pole). The vector is the *difference* between those two sets inside the model."
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": "# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# PERSONA ANCHOR BANK\n# Each persona: hinglish_anchors (positive pole) + english_anchors (negative pole)\n# In production, load these from your persona store / Weaviate. Here they're inline.\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nPERSONAS = {\n    \"rahul\": {\n        \"description\": \"young gamer, heavy Hinglish slang\",\n        \"hinglish_anchors\": [\n            \"yaar kya scene hai deployment ka, kuch pata?\",\n            \"bhai kal meeting confirm hua ya nahi?\",\n            \"server down tha, abhi theek ho gaya lagta hai\",\n            \"chal koi na, phir try karte hain\",\n            \"update de bhai jaldi, hum wait kar rahe hain\",\n            \"kya lag raha hai, ho jayega aaj?\",\n            \"arre yaar sab set hai, tension mat le\",\n            \"bhai bata na kya hua, suspense mat rakh\",\n        ],\n        \"english_anchors\": [\n            \"What is the status of the deployment, do you have any update?\",\n            \"Has the meeting for tomorrow been confirmed?\",\n            \"The server was down but it appears to be working now.\",\n            \"That's alright, we will try again.\",\n            \"Please share the update quickly, we are waiting.\",\n            \"How does it look, will it be done today?\",\n            \"Everything is set, no need to worry.\",\n            \"Please let me know what happened, don't keep me in suspense.\",\n        ],\n    },\n    \"priya\": {\n        \"description\": \"urban professional, light code-mix\",\n        \"hinglish_anchors\": [\n            \"okay so deployment ho gaya, good to go hai?\",\n            \"meeting is confirmed, right? let me know.\",\n            \"server issue resolve ho gaya, finally.\",\n            \"no worries, we'll handle it.\",\n            \"please update karo when it's live.\",\n            \"timeline thoda tight hai, manage hoga?\",\n            \"sab sorted hai from my end.\",\n            \"just confirm karo, rest I'll take care of.\",\n        ],\n        \"english_anchors\": [\n            \"The deployment is complete, are we good to go?\",\n            \"Is the meeting confirmed? Please let me know.\",\n            \"The server issue has been resolved, finally.\",\n            \"No worries, we will handle it.\",\n            \"Please update me when it goes live.\",\n            \"The timeline is a bit tight, will we manage?\",\n            \"Everything is sorted from my end.\",\n            \"Just confirm and I will take care of the rest.\",\n        ],\n    },\n    \"ankit\": {\n        \"description\": \"small-town, Hindi-dominant\",\n        \"hinglish_anchors\": [\n            \"bhai deployment ki kya situation hai abhi?\",\n            \"kal ki meeting ka koi news hai?\",\n            \"server wala kaam ho gaya kya?\",\n            \"koi baat nahi, fir dekh lenge\",\n            \"bata do jaldi, hum idhar hain\",\n            \"kal tak ho jayega na?\",\n            \"sab theek hai, chinta mat karo\",\n            \"arrey bata do kya hua\",\n        ],\n        \"english_anchors\": [\n            \"What is the current situation with the deployment?\",\n            \"Is there any news about tomorrow's meeting?\",\n            \"Has the server work been completed?\",\n            \"It's okay, we will look into it later.\",\n            \"Please inform me quickly, I am here.\",\n            \"It will be done by tomorrow, right?\",\n            \"Everything is fine, don't worry.\",\n            \"Please tell me what happened.\",\n        ],\n    },\n}\n\nprint(\"Personas loaded:\", list(PERSONAS.keys()))"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "## 2. Build Dataset + Train a Vector Per Persona\n\nFor each persona, we pair every Hinglish anchor with every English anchor (cross-product). More pairs = cleaner vector. We then train with repeng's PCA — same cheap pass, no gradient."
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": "def make_dataset(persona_data: dict) -> list:\n    \"\"\"Build contrastive pairs from persona's own anchors.\"\"\"\n    hinglish = persona_data[\"hinglish_anchors\"]\n    english  = persona_data[\"english_anchors\"]\n    pairs = []\n    for h in hinglish:\n        for e in english:\n            pos_msgs = [\n                {\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"You are a warm, casual assistant who speaks Hinglish.\"},\n                {\"role\": \"user\",   \"content\": h},\n            ]\n            neg_msgs = [\n                {\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"You are a formal professional assistant who speaks pure English.\"},\n                {\"role\": \"user\",   \"content\": e},\n            ]\n            pos = tokenizer.apply_chat_template(pos_msgs, tokenize=False, add_generation_prompt=True)\n            neg = tokenizer.apply_chat_template(neg_msgs, tokenize=False, add_generation_prompt=True)\n            pairs.append(DatasetEntry(positive=pos, negative=neg))\n    return pairs\n\nvectors = {}  # persona_name -> ControlVector\nOUT_DIR = Path(\"/kaggle/working/persona_vectors\")\nOUT_DIR.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\n\nfor name, data in PERSONAS.items():\n    print(f\"\\n=== Training vector for: {name} ({data['description']}) ===")\n    ds = make_dataset(data)\n    print(f\"  contrastive pairs: {len(ds)}\")\n    model.reset()\n    vec = ControlVector.train(model, tokenizer, ds)\n    vectors[name] = vec\n    # Save: .pt file per persona\n    torch.save(vec.directions, OUT_DIR / f\"{name}_tone_vector.pt\")\n    print(f\"  saved → {OUT_DIR}/{name}_tone_vector.pt\")\n\nprint(\"\\nAll vectors trained.\")\nprint(\"Output files:\", list(OUT_DIR.iterdir()))"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "## 3. Test Each Persona's Dial — Same Prompt, Three Coefficients\n\nThe real test: the *same* deployment update prompt should sound **differently casual** for Rahul vs Priya vs Ankit at `coeff +2`. If Rahul sounds more slangy than Priya, the persona-specific vectors are working."
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": "def generate(prompt: str, vector: ControlVector, coeff: float, max_new: int = 80) -> str:\n    model.reset()\n    if coeff != 0:\n        model.set_control(vector, coeff)\n    msgs = [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": prompt}]\n    ids = tokenizer.apply_chat_template(msgs, return_tensors=\"pt\", add_generation_prompt=True).to(model.device)\n    out = model.generate(\n        ids,\n        max_new_tokens=max_new,\n        do_sample=False,\n        repetition_penalty=1.3,\n        pad_token_id=tokenizer.eos_token_id,\n    )\n    model.reset()\n    return tokenizer.decode(out[0, ids.shape[-1]:], skip_special_tokens=True).strip()\n\nPROMPT = \"Can you give me an update on the deployment?\"\n\nfor name, vec in vectors.items():\n    print(f\"\\n{'='*55}\")\n    print(f\"PERSONA: {name} — {PERSONAS[name]['description']}\")\n    print(f\"{'='*55}\")\n    for c in [-2.0, 0.0, 2.0]:\n        label = {-2.0: \"formal (-2)\", 0.0: \"baseline (0)\", 2.0: \"persona Hinglish (+2)\"}[c]\n        print(f\"\\n  [{label}]\")\n        print(f\"  {generate(PROMPT, vec, c)}\")\n"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "## 4. Inspect: Are the Persona Vectors Actually Different?\n\nThis cell measures the cosine similarity between each pair of persona vectors at a representative layer. High similarity (~0.9+) means the vectors are essentially the same axis — persona-specificity is weak. Low similarity (~0.5–0.7) means each persona really does occupy its own direction — we want this."
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": "import torch.nn.functional as F\nfrom itertools import combinations\n\n# Pick a representative middle layer to compare.\nREP_LAYER = list(vectors[\"rahul\"].directions.keys())[len(vectors[\"rahul\"].directions) // 2]\nprint(f\"Comparing vectors at layer {REP_LAYER}\\n\")\n\nfor a, b in combinations(list(vectors.keys()), 2):\n    va = torch.tensor(vectors[a].directions[REP_LAYER], dtype=torch.float32)\n    vb = torch.tensor(vectors[b].directions[REP_LAYER], dtype=torch.float32)\n    sim = F.cosine_similarity(va.unsqueeze(0), vb.unsqueeze(0)).item()\n    interp = \"nearly identical (weak persona-specificity)\" if sim > 0.90 else (\n              \"some overlap\" if sim > 0.75 else \"good separation — persona-specific\")\n    print(f\"  {a} ↔ {b}: cosine = {sim:.3f}  →  {interp}\")\n"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "## 5. Download Your Persona Vectors\n\nAfter the notebook finishes, download the `.pt` files from `/kaggle/working/persona_vectors/`. These are the reusable tone directions — one per persona. In the production Vachan serving layer (Path-B), the inference server loads these at startup, stores them in a dict keyed by persona ID, then calls `model.set_control(vectors[persona_id], coeff)` at generation time based on the fidelity ring reading."
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": "# List what was saved.\nprint(\"Saved files:\")\nfor f in sorted(OUT_DIR.iterdir()):\n    size_kb = f.stat().st_size / 1024\n    print(f\"  {f.name}  ({size_kb:.1f} KB)\")\n\nprint(\"\\nDownload these from the Kaggle output panel (right sidebar → Output).\")\n"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "## 6. What to Check + Next Steps\n\n### Green signals\n- `coeff +2` for Rahul sounds slangier than `coeff +2` for Priya — persona-specificity is real.\n- Cosine similarities between persona vectors are < 0.80 — they occupy distinct directions.\n- `coeff 0` (baseline) sounds the same across all personas — the base model is untouched.\n\n### If persona vectors are too similar (cosine > 0.90)\n- Add more anchors per persona (aim for 16–24 each).\n- Make anchors more contrasting — pick the most persona-distinctive phrases, not generic ones.\n- Widen the layer band: `range(-3, -22, -1)`.\n\n### If coeff +2 degenerates (gibberish)\n- Back off to `coeff 1.5` — high coefficients destabilize 4-bit quant models more than full precision.\n\n### Path into Vachan production (later sprints)\n1. **Store vectors**: upload the `.pt` files to your serving infra, keyed by `persona_id`.\n2. **Serving hook**: at inference, the PFS gate reads `av_cosine`. If it drops below threshold for a persona, switch from Groq → local model, load `vectors[persona_id]`, set coeff via fidelity ring reading.\n3. **Coeff tuning**: use the Fidelity Ring's `av_cosine` as the objective. Binary search coeff (1.0, 1.5, 2.0) on a small validation set per persona.\n4. **Sarvam-30B option**: same notebook, swap MODEL to a Sarvam mirror. Needs 2×T4 on Kaggle. Probably better native Hinglish baseline — worth a separate spike."
  }
 ],
